==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{confix|en|anthropo|sophy}}, from a Renaissance Latin {{m|la|anthroposophia}} (attested in Heinrich Cornelius Agrippa, d. 1535, and Thomas Vaughan, d. 1666), popularized from the 1910s via German {{m|de|Anthroposophie}} ([[:w:Rudolf Steiner|Rudolf Steiner]], 1861–1925).

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm}} {{IPA|en|/ˌænθɹəˈpɑsəfi/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˌænθɹəˈpɒsəfi/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|archaic|as used before Rudolf Steiner}} Knowledge or understanding of human nature.
# A spiritual movement inaugurated by Rudolf Steiner (also capitalized as {{m|en|Anthroposophy}}), postulating the existence of an objective, intellectually comprehensible spiritual world accessible to direct experience through inner development.
#*'''1975''', {{w|Saul Bellow}}, ''Humboldt's Gift'' [Avon ed., 1976, p. 223]:
#*: '''Anthroposophy''' was having definite effects. I couldn't take any of this to heart. Other-worldliness tinged it all and every little while my spirit seemed to dissociate itself.

